  "resources": {
    "costumbres": {
      "c-289e646296dfb404": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-331",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The Mosaic law gave the creditor the right to claim the person and children of the debtor who could not pay that they, might serve him until the year of Jubilee, when they again became free. See Leviticus 25: 39-41. Reference is made to this custom in Nehemiah 5:5,8; Job 24:9; Is…"
      },
      "c-336e3b8da6dc9c15": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-333",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Such a room makes a desirable place of retirement for the master of the house. Ahaziah was in an aliyah, in his palace of Samaria, when he fell through the lattice-work of the window and injured himself (2 Kings 1:2). Eglon, King of Moab, was in a room of this description when he…"
      },
      "c-3c29d37605360cca": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-334",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Ladies of the higher class in the East seldom walk, but almost always ride on asses, which are there more frequently used for riding than with us. The rider is attended by a servant who runs behind, and, with a whip or stick, drives or goads the animal forward at whatever pace ma…"
      },
      "c-5dd959204dd09cc6": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-332",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Asuk, pot, is supposed to have been an earthen jar, deep and narrow, with a pointed bottom which was inserted into a stand of wood or stone, or stuck into the ground like the Roman and Egyptian amphora. Phillott (Smith's Dictionary of the Bible, s. v. Pot) thinks that the asuk ha…"
      },
      "c-824a73adc43045f3": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-336",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "These are merely the customary formal salutations which are so profusely used by Orientals. Dr. Thomson says, “If you ask after a person whom you know to be sick, the reply at first will invariably be well, thank God, even when the next sentence is to inform you that he is dying”…"
      },
      "c-d79591cd473897b2": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-335",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The prophets were probably accustomed at the new moon and on the Sabbath-day to assemble the people for instruction and edification. The question of the husband of the Shunammite woman appears, therefore, to express his astonishment that she should go to the prophet at a time whi…"
      }
    },
